        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: शय्या",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "शय्या (śayyā) — \"a bed\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "शय्या (śayyā) comes from śī, \"to lie down\", so a bed is simply the lying-down place."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its cousins settle rather than sleep: Greek keitai, \"he lies\"; Latin cīvis, a citizen — one who lies down in a place instead of moving on — behind city, civil and civilian; and English home, and hide, once a measure of land a single family could settle. A bed and a city turn out to be one idea at two sizes. Ends in -आ, like लता."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five things in a house, and a bed with a city inside it."
            }
          ]
        },
